That's it. The fix: pass `e` (not e<<20) to tsort_range, or compare against (tsort[m]>>20) properly. Let me fix it: tsort_range((uint64_t)e, &lo, &hi) — comparing (tsort[m]>>20) [32-bit tgt] with target [32-bit e]. ✓.

Let me also double-check the lower-bound logic of the binary search:
- lo: first index with (tsort[m]>>20) >= target. Standard: while l<h: m=(l+h)/2; if (tsort[m]>>20) < target → l=m+1 else h=m. ✓.
- hi: first index with (tsort[m]>>20) > target. ✓.

Good. And the extraction of k3 = tsort[j] & 0xFFFFF ✓ (lower 20 bits = k3).

So the only bug was the <<20 in the tsort_range call. (The v1 and v2 code both had `tsort_range((uint64_t)e<<20, ...)` — yes, I wrote it that way.)

Let me also reconsider the v3 run: it was killed at 360s due to the progress printing disaster. The actual scan is ~25-40s. I need to remove the broken progress printing (or fix the condition) and rerun.

